package helptopics

const hostAdminTopic = `The host commands read the local users, groups and folder permissions and change them through registered actions. Reading changes nothing and runs without sudo for root and for the administrators of the group qhost, everyone else gets one line and exit code 1. Changing needs root.

Reading:
  qhost host users
  qhost host user <name>
  qhost host groups
  qhost host group <name>
  qhost host folder <path>
  sudo qhost host file <path>

A user or group is a system user or group when its ID lies outside 1000 to 60000, so root and nobody count as system accounts. A user is an admin in the group sudo, a docker user in the group docker, and enabled in the group qhost. A group lists the users whose primary group it is as members.

Whether an account is locked or expired and which SSH keys it has are only readable as root. Otherwise they are unknown: absent in JSON and 'unknown' in the other formats. Keys are shown as type, comment and SHA256 fingerprint, never as the key itself.

host file reads as root and changes nothing: a folder is listed with name, kind (file, folder, link or other), size, owner, group, permissions and modification time of each entry, a link with its target as text. A file shows the same and its content; a file over 1 MiB or with binary content (a NUL byte or no valid UTF-8) shows only that, with too_large or binary set and an empty content. A link is never followed, neither on the way nor as the path itself.

A folder or file shows owner, group and the level of owner, group and others: nothing, read, or read and write. For a directory read and write both need the right to enter it; write without read counts as nothing. It also shows whether ACLs are set, the special bits setuid, setgid and sticky, and whether the path is protected: /, /etc, /usr, /boot, /proc, /sys and everything below the last five. Links and .. are resolved before that check.

Changing:
  sudo qhost host apply <action> [args...] --confirm
  sudo qhost host undo <journal-id> --confirm
  sudo qhost host journal

apply and undo ask no question. Without --confirm they show the sentence and the exact commands on standard error, change nothing, read no secret input, and exit with code 2; a secret input is shown as a placeholder. With --confirm they run. sudo qhost host apply --proposal <id> applies a proposal of the host instead of an action typed by hand, with the same journal, check, restore, protection rules and report; it is described in qhost help proposals. A change first records the previous state in the journal, then runs its commands, checks the result and, when a later command or the check fails, restores the previous state. A first command that fails changed nothing and is not taken back. The report shows the sentence, the exact commands, the check and the result applied, failed or restored, and a failed or restored change ends with exit code 1. A protection rule refuses a change before anything runs, shows the reason with the result refused and ends with exit code 1; it leaves no journal entry. Put -- before the action when an argument starts with a dash.

The journal lives in /var/lib/qhost-host/journal (folder 0700, files 0600, readable by root only), holds the latest 50 changes and, for each, what an undo would do. Secrets, passwords and key texts are read from standard input only, never from an argument, and appear nowhere in the report, the journal or the action log. Each change is reported to the host service, so qhost audit lists it with the action, the journal entry and the person behind sudo (SUDO_UID). When the host service is not running the change still stands, and the report warns that the audit entry is missing.

Actions (a secret input is marked with stdin):
  user.create <name> [admin] [docker] [group=<name>]...  (stdin: JSON {"password": "...", "keys": ["..."]}, keys optional)
  user.create-service <name> [home] [docker] [group=<name>]...
  user.set-password <name> [must-change]  (stdin: the new password)
  user.set-admin <name> on|off [without-qhost]
  user.set-docker <name> on|off
  user.enable <name>
  user.lock <name>, user.unlock <name>
  user.delete <name> [with-home]
  user.add-groups <name> <group>..., user.remove-groups <name> <group>...
  user.add-key <name>  (stdin: one public key line)
  user.remove-key <name> <SHA256 fingerprint>
  group.create <name>, group.delete <name>, group.rename <old> <new>
  group.set-members <group> +<user>|-<user>...
  folder.set-permissions <path> <owner> <group> <user-level> <group-level> <other-level> [recursive] [system-path]  (levels: nothing, read, read-write)
  file.write <path> [system-path]  (stdin: the content, text up to 1 MiB)
  file.create-folder <path> [system-path]
  file.rename <source> <target> [system-path]
  file.copy <source> <target> [permanent] [system-path]
  file.remove <path> [permanent] [system-path]

File actions: the path is absolute and clean. file.write writes an existing file in place, so its inode, owner, group, permissions and hard links stay, and creates a missing file in an existing folder with the owner and group of that folder; the content is never an argument and appears nowhere but in the journal. file.create-folder creates one folder in an existing folder the same way. No link is followed, on the way or at the end: a link in the path is refused. Nothing is written in a .git folder, in .qatlas/qhost of a stack folder or in /var/lib/qhost-host, which holds the journal of the host changes and is the way back; /proc and /sys are never written, and any other system path needs system-path. Content over 1 MiB or binary content is refused, and so is a file that already holds either. Undo puts the previous content, owner and permissions back, or removes what the change created, only as long as the file is unchanged since; a folder is removed only while empty. file.rename moves within one file system and mount, and the target must not exist; its undo moves it back while the target is still the renamed entry, the same inode created at the same time (the birth time, where the file system reports it, else the change time of its metadata, so a later change of owner, mode or link count refuses the undo), and the source is free. file.copy and file.remove work on a file or a folder with everything below it. A copy keeps links as links, takes owner and group from the target folder and the mode of the source without setuid, setgid and sticky bit, appears only when complete, and refuses devices, FIFOs and sockets. The plan names the number of entries and the size. Up to 5000 entries and 10 MiB the undo of a copy removes it while it is unchanged, each entry with the same inode, content and creation time, and the undo of a remove builds the tree again while the path is free, with content, links, owner, group, mode and time; hard links come back as separate files, ACLs and extended attributes do not. Larger trees are refused unless permanent is given: then the change is final, the journal keeps nothing, and there is no undo; more than 100000 entries are refused in any case. A folder that holds .git, .qatlas/qhost or the journal folder is not removed or renamed, and one that holds .git or .qatlas/qhost is not copied, copying out of one is allowed. A system folder such as /etc or /usr is never removed, renamed or taken as a target. The file actions cannot be proposed.

Protection rules: nobody takes administrator rights or access to qhost from themselves, locks or deletes their own account, and the last administrator stays; the groups sudo and qhost are neither renamed nor deleted; a user with running processes and a group that is the primary group of a user are not deleted; a system path is changed only with system-path and never together with everything below it. Lock is the normal way to stop someone: it blocks every login, also by SSH key, while running sessions stay open. Deleting a user keeps the home by default and can be undone, which creates the account again with the same UID, groups and password; with-home deletes the home and mail spool and cannot be undone.`

const hostTUITopic = `System shows the local users and groups of this host on the sub-tabs Users and Groups, its files and the permissions of a folder on Folders, the proposals of administrators on Proposals, the latest changes of the host on Changes and the action log of the host service on Log. It reads the account files and the file system of the host, so it works without the deploy service and without sudo. What only root can read, the login state and the SSH keys of a user, shows as unknown unless the interface runs with sudo. Admin means the groups sudo and qhost together; an administrator without the group qhost shows as "yes, without qhost" and has no access to qhost yet. Docker means the group docker, which is practically root. System accounts and system groups, whose ID lies outside 1000 to 60000, are listed in a group of their own below the regular ones. r reads the users and groups again.

Every change shows a sentence in plain words, the exact commands dimmed below it, and y or n. y hands the terminal to sudo qhost host apply, which asks for the password itself; qhost never sees it. Started with sudo, the interface runs the command directly. A password or SSH key is typed into a hidden field and reaches the command on standard input only, never a command line or the screen. The result names the journal entry, and Changes takes the change back (see qhost help host-admin). The action log of the host service records every change with the person behind sudo, never with a password or key: Log and qhost audit list it, and qhost audit verify checks the chain of hashes that links its entries, where a broken chain means an entry was changed or removed.

Changes of root, of system accounts and of system groups ask in red and name the consequence. Only what cuts off the way back is refused: taking administrator rights from yourself, locking or deleting yourself, leaving no administrator with access to qhost, and deleting or renaming the groups sudo and qhost.

On Users, a creates a user with an initial password that must be changed at the first login, optionally as administrator, with Docker, in further groups and with SSH keys. e or enter offers the changes of the marked user, one at a time: access to qhost for an administrator without it, administrator on or off, Docker on or off, add to or remove from groups (several at once), a new password, add or remove an SSH key, lock and unlock. Lock is the normal way to stop someone: it blocks every login, also by SSH key, while running sessions stay open. x deletes the marked user after asking whether its home goes too: keeping the home is the default and can be undone, deleting the home and the mail spool cannot. A user with running processes is not deleted.

On Groups, a creates a group, e or enter adds and removes members of the marked group (several users at once with one confirmation; in sudo, adding also gives access to qhost and removing takes both), n renames it and x deletes it, each after a confirmation. A user whose primary group it is can neither be added nor removed, and such a group is not deleted. Deleting can be undone, which creates the group again with the same group number and members. Renaming always asks in red, because configurations such as sudoers address a group by its name and keep the old one; changes in the groups root and docker and in system groups ask in red too.

Folders browses the files of the host from the root /: enter opens a folder or shows a file, / filters the entries, esc goes up, and every path can be reached. A link shows its target and is never followed. A text file up to 1 MiB is shown read-only with line numbers (#), wrapping (w), search (/) and syntax colours for YAML, env, JSON, INI, TOML, shell and Dockerfiles; a binary file or a larger one is named, not shown. What your own permissions allow is read directly; anything else asks first with the sentence and the exact command sudo qhost host file, and y hands the terminal to sudo, which asks for the password itself. Started with sudo, the interface reads everything directly. e (edit) opens the marked file, or the file shown, in your own editor: the one set under Settings > General, else VISUAL, EDITOR, then micro, nano or vi. The editor never runs as root: started with sudo it runs as the user behind sudo, and started as root without sudo editing is refused. It edits a private copy in memory (XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, /run/user/<uid> or a private folder under /dev/shm) that is removed afterwards; an editor qhost does not know may leave backup or swap files, so it asks first. A file only root may read is edited only where that copy lies in memory. Folders, links, binary files, files over 1 MiB and anything inside a .git folder are not edited. An unchanged text writes nothing; a YAML, JSON or .env file is checked first, and a failed check offers to edit again or to discard. Writing back asks with the sentence and the exact command of sudo qhost host apply file.write, which writes the file in place with its owner, group and mode, never through a link, and can be undone; the text goes only to its standard input, a system path asks in red. n creates an empty file or a folder, r renames, c copies and d removes the marked entry; the name is asked for in the same folder, and the changes are the root actions file.write, file.create-folder, file.rename, file.copy and file.remove, each after a question with the sentence and the exact command. Nothing that exists is replaced, a name holds no / and is not . or .., a link is never followed and a .git folder is only read. A folder is counted before it is copied or removed, and the question names its entries and the size of its files; up to 5000 entries and 10 MiB it can be undone, beyond that the question is red, says that it is final and the command gets the option permanent. A folder only root may read is not counted, which the question says. After a change the folder is read again, except a folder read through sudo, which R reads again so that no second password question comes unasked. i (permissions) shows for the marked entry the owner, the group and what each of them and everyone else may do: nothing, read (open and list), or read and write; an ACL or a special bit (setuid, setgid, sticky) is named but not changed. e changes owner, group and the three levels of a folder, optionally for everything below it, except inside a .git folder, which is only read here: the confirmation then names the number of entries, counted before anything runs, and a folder with more than 5000 entries or an entry that cannot be read is refused. A system path (/, /etc, /usr, /boot, /proc, /sys and everything below them) asks in red; /proc and /sys are never changed, and a system path itself is never changed together with everything below it. Undoing restores the owner, group and mode of every entry the change touched. Q on the row of a stack folder in Stacks, or in the stack detail, opens the same browser with the stack folder as its root, which esc at the root leaves, and without permissions. .git and .qatlas/qhost are only read there; the forms of the stack detail change the settings. The values of secrets.env show masked until V uncovers them. Saving commits nothing, and = there opens the uncommitted changes of the folder and @ its history (see qhost help sources); saving secrets.env takes effect at the next deploy, which the interface offers at once, without sudo through sudo qhost stack deploy. A change in the data folder of a running container of the folder asks with a warning but is not refused.

Proposals lists the proposals of changes to the host that administrators submitted, newest first, with their state. It reads them from the host service like Log, so it needs neither sudo nor the deploy service, and reads them again after a change and on r. enter shows a proposal: who proposed it, its state, the sentence in plain words, the exact commands dimmed as they would be typed by hand, and a proposed SSH key with its type, comment and fingerprint. The view is marked red where the change gives the Docker right, which means full control over the host, enables access to qhost, or touches root, a system account, a system group or a system path. A approves an open proposal with the same question as a change typed by hand, the sentence and the exact commands, and y runs sudo qhost host apply --proposal with the fingerprint that was shown: the root command reads the proposal again, checks the rules that apply to the host, makes the change with a journal entry that Changes can undo, and marks the proposal as applied. For a new user the interface asks for the initial password first, in a hidden field; it reaches the command on standard input only. N rejects an open proposal after a question and runs sudo qhost proposal reject. The notice at the start counts the open proposals of the host service and of the deploy service; one that cannot be reached counts for none.

Changes lists the latest 50 changes of the host from the journal, newest first, with time, the person behind sudo, the sentence, the result and whether an undo is possible; below the list stand the sentence and the exact commands of the undo of the marked change. Only root can read the journal: started with sudo the interface reads it on its own, otherwise r reads it through sudo qhost host journal, so no password prompt appears unasked. enter on a change asks to undo it with the same question as a change, the sentence and the exact commands dimmed below it, and y runs sudo qhost host undo. An undo cannot itself be undone, so its question says so and its yes is red. A change that cannot be undone, such as a deleted home, one that did not stand and one undone already say why instead of asking. After a change made from the interface the page says that the journal is older than the host until r reads it again.

Log shows the latest 200 entries of the action log of the host service, newest first, with time, user, action, result and the change of the host with its journal entry, under the result of qhost audit verify: intact with the number of days and entries checked, or broken with the day, the line and the kind of damage. Every request to the host service is an entry of this log itself, so the page reads it when it opens for the first time, after a change made from the interface and on r, never on a timer. qhost audit filters it further.`
